## JSON Schemas for Structured Output

Calling a tool with a JSON schema is the sturdiest route to structured output.
It pins down the **shape** but not the **meaning**:

- Output is always well-formed JSON — braces close, no stray commas.
- The structure is enforced, so required fields are present.
- Values are **not** fact-checked, though — they can still be wrong.

### Schema design rules

- **Required vs optional** — mark a field required only when it is genuinely
  always present; otherwise the model may invent a value to fill the gap.
- **Allow nulls** — `"type": ["string", "null"]` lets the model return `null`
  for missing data instead of hallucinating one.
- **Open enums** — pair your categories with an `"other"` option plus a
  free-text detail so anything outside the list is not silently dropped.
- **An `"unclear"` enum value** — give the model an honest escape hatch when it
  cannot confidently classify; better than a confident wrong label.

