## Claude Code — `CLAUDE.md` Hierarchy

Three levels of instruction:

| Level         | Location                                  | Applies to                | Committed? | Typical content                                       |
| ------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| **User**      | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`                     | You alone                 | Never      | Personal style and preferences                        |
| **Project**   | `.claude/CLAUDE.md` or a root `CLAUDE.md` | Everyone on the repo      | Yes        | Coding and testing standards, architectural decisions |
| **Directory** | A `CLAUDE.md` inside a subfolder          | While editing files there | Yes        | Conventions local to that part of the tree            |

> ⚠️ **Classic trap:** putting project instructions in the user-level file, so
> new teammates never receive them.

### `@path` imports

`CLAUDE.md` can pull in other files with `@path`, keeping configuration modular:

- Both relative and absolute paths are supported.
- Imports nest up to **5 levels** deep.

### The `.claude/rules/` directory

A topic-by-topic alternative to one monolithic `CLAUDE.md` — individual `.md`
files. The real lever is **YAML frontmatter with a `paths` glob**: a rule loads
only when Claude edits a file matching the pattern, which saves context and
tokens.

- Use **`paths` rules** when a convention spans files scattered across the tree
  (tests, migrations).
- Use a **directory-level `CLAUDE.md`** when the convention is tied to one
  specific folder.

